    8	---
    9	
    10	## Proposal A — Tropical Hankel Rank of π
    11	
    12	### Idea
    13	
    14	Build the Hankel-like matrix
    15	$$H \in \mathbb{N}^{m \times m}, \qquad H_{i,j} \;=\; \pi(i+j),\quad 0 \le i, j < m,$$
    16	where $m = \lfloor N/2 \rfloor$. Treat $H$ as a matrix over the
    17	**tropical (max-plus)** semiring $\mathbb R \cup \{-\infty\}$ with
    18	$\oplus = \max$, $\otimes = +$.
    19	
    20	The **tropical rank** $r(H)$ is the smallest $r$ such that $H = U \otimes V$
    21	with $U \in (\mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\})^{m \times r}$,
    22	$V \in (\mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\})^{r\times m}$. In contrast to ordinary linear
    23	algebra, tropically low rank means $\pi(i+j) = \max_k (u_{ik} + v_{kj})$.
    24	Such a decomposition is exactly a piecewise-linear concave-in-each-variable
    25	representation — and the prime-counting function is asymptotically piecewise
    26	linear (with breakpoints at primes).
    27	
    28	If $r(H_N)$ scales like $\mathrm{polylog}(N)$, then primes can be located by
    29	a sequence of $r$ comparisons of linear forms; combined with binary search,
    30	this gives $O(\mathrm{polylog}\, N)$ evaluation of $\pi(x)$, hence $p(n)$
